A||||11||GT Bastard||Who's running 3.1 or 3.2 with stock Fiero heads?||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||07-14-2000||08:22 PM||||If so, what kind of power are you getting?  I know a couple forum members are using the hypereutectic replacement pistons for the 3.1  These pistons have a slight dish.  BUT, would it be a bad idea to use these pistons with the stock Fiero heads?  These are the pistons I've ordered, so I'm concerned.  This thread sparked my interest:  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002556.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002556.html</A> <P>Also:  A friend of mine has a 3.1 motor straight from an older chevy van(non-al head.) The motor went DIRECTLY from the van into his car with nothing but new bearings(stock cam and all...)  His car is wicked fast!  Until 1FST2M6's ex-car showed up, it was the fastest Fiero here(and we have several nicely modified Fiero's here along with dozens of stock V6's.)  The very same guy just finished installing a 3.1(once again from a chevy van) in someone else's '86 GT 4sp.  The only difference is, THIS car has a Compucam 2030 and stock Fiero heads.  Anyhow, Brad did an excellent job of installing this setup and the car runs great.  It's slightly better than stock, but it's not nearly as fast as Brads 3.1 from the van.  Same trannies and all... Brad has tried different proms, played with timing, etc and still can't get it to perform near his 3.1  Could this be because he's using the stock heads from the Van?  I mean, was his van motor just really well matched?  We can't figure out why this isn't working. Any imput appreciated... TIA||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||07-14-2000||09:31 PM||||All the cast iron 60-degree v6 heads have the same size combustion chambers. The only difference is that the H.O. heads (fiero) have bigger valves than the standard heads.  <P>On Brads 3.1 liter from the van being faster than the built 3.1. For one the built 3.1 is going to have much less bottom-end torque than the stock one because of the more aggressive Crain 2030 cam. The built 3.1 will have better top end. I know that with the 3.4 swaps (which are no different than a 3.1-3.2) that they have fuel mixture problems especially as the Rpm’s get higher (some say they run lean and some say they run rich). When you start changing things on the 3.4 (remember they are similar to 3.1) it gets harder to tune the engine so that it performs the way it should according to Ed Parks who has been doing 3.4 swaps in his fiero factory for a long time. <P>I am doing the 3.4 swap and I will definitely have to get a custom prom for this engine so that I don’t run into the same problems with the fuel mixture. I would think that the 3.1 motor from the van is so mild that it can work with the 2.8 prom with no major problems. The fact that this thing is probably a torque monster because it was designed to pull around a 3500-pound van makes it really fast in a street race.  If you friends built 3.1 engine is sound then it is probably in need of proper tuning were the exhaust gas is analyzed to see if it is running rich or lean from idle to 6000 rpm. If the built 3.1 motor’s compression ratio was to low than that would hurt the power output also. The 3.4 Camaro engine has a 9.0 compression ratio and when you perform a compression test on these engines they are usually around 180 psi in each cylinder. If your friends compression ratio were about the same than I would think that under the same test it would be around 180 psi also. The 3.4 and the 3.1 have the same stroke crankshaft but the 3.4 bore is .120 bigger than a stock 3.1 engine. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-15-2000||12:24 AM||||If... You ran a Carb'd set-up with the 3.4L(camaroo) ,instead of the Fuel Injection, Does that ELIMINATE ALOT of the problem's getting low end tourqe??  BTW, Does the 2.8 head's fit the 3.4L or should I stay with stock 3.4L(camaroo)head's??  Does this make any sense??      ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||07-15-2000||12:58 AM||||The Camaro heads and the Fiero heads are supposed to be identical.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||07-15-2000||12:00 PM||||The fiero heads and the 3.4 heads are the same heads so you could use either one. <P>1FieroGTRacingInc, putting a carburetor setup on the 3.4 in place of the tuned port injection that the fiero comes with would make tuning the 3.4 a lot easier and less expensive. The only problem with that is it may not be emissions legal. The great thing about tuned port injection is that it produces great low-end torque and gives great fuel economy and it also gives you better cold engine operation. The carburetor setup wound not give you as much low-end torque as the tuned port injection but it would produce better high rpm power than tuned port injection. TPI produces better low-end torque because of the long runners that it has which produce better low-end torque.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-15-2000||02:56 PM||||88 Formula,  Could I somewhat control some of my low-end torque by getting a good intake that is for making low-end torque(ex. the Edelbrock... Performer i think) I live in Texas so cold startup's probably would be rare, as i plan on parking it from Dec.-Feb.???  Make any sense??  Steve||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||07-15-2000||06:14 PM||||1FieroGTRacingInc, The thing that makes the biggest difference in where the engine produces its power is the camshaft. If you want low-end torque than don't get an aggressive cam. If you want all out horsepower than go with an aggressive camshaft. The nice thing about Edlebrock is that they have complete packages that have already been dynoed so you know how much power to expect and were the engine will produce this power. <P>I would suggest that you get the Desktop Dyno Simulation program like I have and play around with the numbers to see what makes the best power for what you want. This way you can see what the differece is between TPI and a carburetor with different camshafts. It is an accurate dyno simulation program that you use on your computer. It only cost about $40 and it is worth every penny because you don’t want to spend all this money on your engine only to find out that it was not the best combination for your needs. If this fiero is going to be a street machine than I would suggest that you be more concerned with torque than how much horsepower the engine makes. Torque is what launches you off the line in a hurry.<P>If you need camshaft specifications I can give them to you by e-mail.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-16-2000||01:19 AM||||Thank's 88Formula.....  I'll get the desktop dyno this weekend..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||07-17-2000||08:07 PM||||1FieroGTRacing-<P>I'm in the process of installing an Edelbrock setup on my 2.8 right now. Shoud have it back on the road soon. I know it's of course not going to be the same as the 3.1, even though it's been bored .040 over, it's still shy of the displacement (not to mention the stroke difference and all) but I'll hopefully be getting a dyno test done shortly after the rebuild is finished. I'll post the results when I'm it's done. <BR>I'm running as aggresive a cam as I could find; .443/288 intake, 465/298 exh. 112 deg centerline. Heads are ported, stock exhaust with obstructions removed. I have Desktop Dyno, which with all the changes I made, indicated a little over 200 hp with a Holley 390 four barrel. Now, if I could only find the 4-71 blower, I know it's around here someplace........<P>BTW, if you decide to get that Edelbrock setup, Jegs is back ordered on the bottom part, and Summit is back ordered on the top. But Summit DOES have the bottom, and Jegs DOES have the top. (just to save you the trouble of searching)<P>Later-<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000009||GT Bastard||07-17-2000||08:10 PM||||Anyhow....<P>Does anyone have any concrete info on the 3.1 heads vs. 2.8 heads?  ||||reg||1
